00:09:38.393 --> 00:09:40.615
because you have Bonobos, right?

00:09:40.615 --> 00:09:42.625
And Bonobos pants are made with

00:09:42.625 --> 00:09:43.629
that contour,

00:09:43.629 --> 00:09:46.070
like curved waistband.

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:47.717
So then he drove to LA, he bought

00:09:47.717 --> 00:09:49.181
a bunch of fabric, he picked

00:09:49.181 --> 00:09:50.889
a really cool pinwale corduroy,

00:09:50.889 --> 00:09:52.780
really thin, really lightweight,

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:53.939
it had great stretch.

00:09:53.939 --> 00:09:55.207
Which is kind of perfect for

00:09:55.207 --> 00:09:56.938
Northern California because it's

00:09:56.938 --> 00:09:58.380
cool in the morning and then it

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:00.660
kind of heats up during the day.

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:02.503
And he just started selling them to

00:10:02.503 --> 00:10:04.164
our classmates for $100.

00:10:04.164 --> 00:10:06.184
And it was like, well, one person

00:10:06.184 --> 00:10:08.066
was wearing them, then five and

00:10:08.066 --> 00:10:09.878
then at some point, half of our

00:10:09.878 --> 00:10:11.773
classmates were buying pants.

00:10:11.773 --> 00:10:13.677
We did a pants party at our house,

00:10:13.677 --> 00:10:15.379
I don't know if anyone has his

00:10:15.379 --> 00:10:16.808
house on El Camino Real,

00:10:16.808 --> 00:10:18.508
used to call the white house,

00:10:18.508 --> 00:10:19.682
anyone live there?

00:10:19.682 --> 00:10:20.709
&gt;&gt; It's still around today.

00:10:20.709 --> 00:10:22.899
&gt;&gt; It's still around, yeah.

00:10:22.899 --> 00:10:24.574
So Brain tried to rebrand it to

00:10:24.574 --> 00:10:26.183
Pleasure Town which didn't

00:10:26.183 --> 00:10:28.060
[INAUDIBLE], but then it went back

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:29.640
to the white house.

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:31.849
Brian is an eccentric character and

00:10:31.849 --> 00:10:33.983
hopefully many of you will meet him

00:10:33.983 --> 00:10:36.050
in MGE or have met him.

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:37.539
And we had a pants party

00:10:37.539 --> 00:10:39.894
where people were playing whatever

00:10:39.894 --> 00:10:41.619
games and long games, and

00:10:41.619 --> 00:10:44.460
we sold $16,000 of pants that day.

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:48.234
And so if you ever have a product

00:10:48.234 --> 00:10:50.476
where you sell that much and

00:10:50.476 --> 00:10:52.435
it's a legal product.

00:10:52.435 --> 00:10:56.109
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; You might be on to

00:10:56.109 --> 00:10:57.162
something.

00:10:57.162 --> 00:10:58.483
&gt;&gt; And you definitely were,

00:10:58.483 --> 00:11:00.645
that is incredible to sell $16,000

00:11:00.645 --> 00:11:02.910
worth of pants in one day.

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:04.938
After the GSB, you decide to work

00:11:04.938 --> 00:11:07.185
on Bonobo's full time, while Brian

00:11:07.185 --> 00:11:08.923
decides to take a job in PE and

00:11:08.923 --> 00:11:10.662
work on Bonobos on nights and

00:11:10.662 --> 00:11:11.910
weekends.

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:13.540
What gave you that conviction?

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:15.137
I know so many of us entrepreneurs

00:11:15.137 --> 00:11:16.507
and aspiring entrepreneurs

00:11:16.507 --> 00:11:17.991
are wondering whether or not we

00:11:17.991 --> 00:11:19.540
should take that leap of faith.

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:23.994
&gt;&gt; I went on a GSB service trip to

00:11:23.994 --> 00:11:26.682
East Africa and it was like 12 or

00:11:26.682 --> 00:11:29.274
13, do you have these service

00:11:29.274 --> 00:11:31.204
learning trips still?

00:11:31.204 --> 00:11:33.050
And we did a homestay for

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:35.633
two nights, and I lived with a guy

00:11:35.633 --> 00:11:37.940
named Stanley and his wife and

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:38.780
two kids.

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:41.356
And it was a clay brick house

00:11:41.356 --> 00:11:44.010
without running water.

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:46.566
They prepared a wonderful meal,

00:11:46.566 --> 00:11:47.752
of course, and

00:11:47.752 --> 00:11:49.943
then there was a big bucket,

00:11:49.943 --> 00:11:52.514
where you dump it over your head.

00:11:52.514 --> 00:11:54.465
And generally it's cold water,

00:11:54.465 --> 00:11:56.067
which is like the ice bucket

00:11:56.067 --> 00:11:56.841
challenge.

00:11:56.841 --> 00:11:58.835
And of course, they found a way to

00:11:58.835 --> 00:12:00.830
heat it, which was wonderful and

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:02.825
it's also like an outhouse where

00:12:02.825 --> 00:12:04.265
you shower or close by.

00:12:04.265 --> 00:12:06.149
And then I came back to

00:12:06.149 --> 00:12:07.718
the white house,

00:12:07.718 --> 00:12:10.448
the one here in El Camino Real.

00:12:10.448 --> 00:12:13.030
And my bathroom you had to walk

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:15.817
outside to get to, and it had two

00:12:15.817 --> 00:12:18.604
showerheads, which is plush,

00:12:18.604 --> 00:12:20.566
and I turned them on and

00:12:20.566 --> 00:12:22.940
the hot water hit me.

00:12:22.940 --> 00:12:24.778
And I had this moment where I was

00:12:24.778 --> 00:12:26.829
like, we do not actually take that

00:12:26.829 --> 00:12:27.550
much risk.

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:31.862
The way that we define it in the US

00:12:31.862 --> 00:12:34.214
for people in the cohort in this

00:12:34.214 --> 00:12:36.839
room, it's actually not risky to

00:12:36.839 --> 00:12:38.210
start a company.

00:12:39.410 --> 00:12:41.158
It's an insult to people whose

00:12:41.158 --> 00:12:43.041
lives are actually risky to think

00:12:43.041 --> 00:12:44.989
of it as risk in similar terms, and

00:12:44.989 --> 00:12:45.729
I don't mean

00:12:45.729 --> 00:12:47.228
that in a judgmental way.

00:12:47.228 --> 00:12:49.244
Many of us have reasons,

00:12:49.244 --> 00:12:52.028
there are medical cost reasons and

00:12:52.028 --> 00:12:54.812
children, and there are things to

00:12:54.812 --> 00:12:57.500
be thoughtful of, but in terms of

00:12:57.500 --> 00:13:00.380
having our future net present value

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:02.410
is in pretty good shape.

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:05.583
And so if you redefine the risk of

00:13:05.583 --> 00:13:07.855
not doing something that you love,

00:13:07.855 --> 00:13:09.747
and that I think is a spiritual

00:13:09.747 --> 00:13:12.030
risk rather than a financial risk.

00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:14.590
And so that trip really changed me.

00:13:14.590 --> 00:13:17.278
And I felt like okay, even though I

00:13:17.278 --> 00:13:19.875
have $3,000 in the bank and

00:13:19.875 --> 00:13:23.121
182,000 of debt, maybe a third of

00:13:23.121 --> 00:13:24.791
which was elective,

00:13:24.791 --> 00:13:27.315
just having fun and traveling.

00:13:27.315 --> 00:13:30.123
I'm probably not going to starve

00:13:30.123 --> 00:13:30.989
one day.

00:13:30.989 --> 00:13:32.496
And with that perspective,

00:13:32.496 --> 00:13:33.498
I was like, okay,

00:13:33.498 --> 00:13:34.704
then what can we go do?

00:13:34.704 --> 00:13:36.420
And I had no good idea of my own,

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:37.608
so I thought, okay,

00:13:37.608 --> 00:13:39.466
I'll just steal Brian's idea.

00:13:39.466 --> 00:13:41.660
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Worked out well.

00:13:41.660 --> 00:13:43.439
That is a helpful way of reframe

00:13:43.439 --> 00:13:45.082
around risk and that reframe

00:13:45.082 --> 00:13:46.793
allowed you to take the leap of

00:13:46.793 --> 00:13:48.504
faith to move to New York, and

00:13:48.504 --> 00:13:50.420
to work on Bonobos.

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:51.839
At the time, you were truly

00:13:51.839 --> 00:13:53.258
a pioneer building a direct

00:13:53.258 --> 00:13:55.640
consumer digitally native brand.

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:58.641
Just to take everyone back to 2007,

00:13:58.641 --> 00:14:01.410
Amazon was irrelevant in fashion.

00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:02.607
People thought e-commerce was

00:14:02.607 --> 00:14:03.790
going to be an afterthought.

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:05.638
And if you were a fashion brand not

00:14:05.638 --> 00:14:07.090
selling through the likes

00:14:07.090 --> 00:14:09.005
of Barneys and Bloomingdale's,

00:14:09.005 --> 00:14:11.120
you were doomed to fail.

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:11.729
So Andy,

00:14:11.729 --> 00:14:13.865
how did you have this aha moment to

00:14:13.865 --> 00:14:16.077
build a digitally native brand and

00:14:16.077 --> 00:14:17.991
how did you convince others?

00:14:17.991 --> 00:14:20.560
&gt;&gt; Well, first, and what is it?

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:22.617
Great entrepreneurs are just

00:14:22.617 --> 00:14:23.360
thieves.

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:24.477
And then second,

00:14:24.477 --> 00:14:25.743
the future is here,

00:14:25.743 --> 00:14:28.060
it's just not evenly distributed.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:30.576
So if you want to build the future,

00:14:30.576 --> 00:14:31.772
don't look to the future,

00:14:31.772 --> 00:14:32.916
look to something that's

00:14:32.916 --> 00:14:34.220
in the corner in the present.

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:37.593
And then kind of past is prologue,

00:14:37.593 --> 00:14:38.863
which is that more or

00:14:38.863 --> 00:14:40.957
less we're the same species last

00:14:40.957 --> 00:14:43.070
200,000 years.

00:14:43.070 --> 00:14:45.109
So there's probably something in

00:14:45.109 --> 00:14:46.938
history that might alert you to

00:14:46.938 --> 00:14:48.001
what was coming.

00:14:48.001 --> 00:14:49.918
For that third thing for me it was

00:14:49.918 --> 00:14:51.907
that I'd worked at Land's End as

00:14:51.907 --> 00:14:54.310
a second year being in AC.

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:56.144
Has anyone spent a couple of

00:14:56.144 --> 00:14:58.705
winters in Dodgeville, Wisconsin?

00:14:58.705 --> 00:15:00.553
It's cold, there's not a lot to

00:15:00.553 --> 00:15:02.500
do other than just work.

00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:04.639
And I got to see this storied

00:15:04.639 --> 00:15:07.429
catalog retailer that didn't have

00:15:07.429 --> 00:15:09.170
stores.

00:15:09.170 --> 00:15:10.978
And I remember one day touring

00:15:10.978 --> 00:15:12.135
the call center and

00:15:12.135 --> 00:15:14.304
seeing all the notes from customers

00:15:14.304 --> 00:15:16.390
that had been mailed in.

00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:18.002
And there was a note from a woman

00:15:18.002 --> 00:15:19.862
that said, dear Elizabeth, thank

00:15:19.862 --> 00:15:21.536
you so much for calling me to wake

00:15:21.536 --> 00:15:24.060
me up the morning of my wedding.

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:26.357
My mom was a mess, my bridesmaids

00:15:26.357 --> 00:15:27.920
were on the sleep end.

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:30.867
You're the best, Carolyn.

00:15:30.867 --> 00:15:32.094
And I was like,

00:15:32.094 --> 00:15:33.973
the paradox of retail is that by

00:15:33.973 --> 00:15:35.203
not having brick and

00:15:35.203 --> 00:15:37.445
mortar stores and going direct, and

00:15:37.445 --> 00:15:39.543
this was the catalog innovation,

00:15:39.543 --> 00:15:41.424
you can actually build a better

00:15:41.424 --> 00:15:43.550
customer service relationship.

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:46.056
And then in terms of what was

00:15:46.056 --> 00:15:47.756
happening in the present that was

00:15:47.756 --> 00:15:49.273
in the corner, there was this

00:15:49.273 --> 00:15:50.560
company called Zappos.

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:52.330
And an entrepreneur named Tony Shea

00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:54.010
and backed by Sequoia.

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:55.245
And it was like, you know startups

00:15:55.245 --> 00:15:56.439
how there's little rumors, you

00:15:56.439 --> 00:15:57.844
don't know exactly what's going on,

00:15:57.844 --> 00:15:58.760
you see that they exist.

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:01.300
And they were selling shoes.

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:03.157
And everyone at the time, this was

00:16:03.157 --> 00:16:05.142
a time where e-commerce people were

00:16:05.142 --> 00:16:06.752
selling cars on eBay Motors.

00:16:06.752 --> 00:16:07.774
But it was like, well,

00:16:07.774 --> 00:16:09.106
you're never going to sell shoes

00:16:09.106 --> 00:16:10.445
because you gotta try them on.

00:16:10.445 --> 00:16:12.345
Wait, don't you have to drive

00:16:12.345 --> 00:16:12.934
the car?

00:16:12.934 --> 00:16:15.764
But Tony had free returns both

00:16:15.764 --> 00:16:19.384
ways, 365 day return policy, and

00:16:19.384 --> 00:16:22.667
a really spirited service team who

00:16:22.667 --> 00:16:25.950
were core to the company culture,

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:26.759
right?

00:16:26.759 --> 00:16:28.486
And that's obviously what

00:16:28.486 --> 00:16:30.438
Delivering Happiness is about

00:16:30.438 --> 00:16:32.388
the book, and the irony of that

00:16:32.388 --> 00:16:34.490
title relative to Tony's fate,

00:16:34.490 --> 00:16:36.516
well, I guess we'll get to that

00:16:36.516 --> 00:16:38.411
type of conversation later.

00:16:38.411 --> 00:16:40.470
So I was like, well, if you can

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:42.847
sell an existing soft goods product

00:16:42.847 --> 00:16:45.225
footwear, then presumably you can

00:16:45.225 --> 00:16:47.602
sell an existing soft goods product

00:16:47.602 --> 00:16:49.980
clothing, and why can't you build

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:51.664
your own brand, right?

00:16:51.664 --> 00:16:53.101
And then I kind of looked at

00:16:53.101 --> 00:16:54.948
the history of the Gap which used

00:16:54.948 --> 00:16:57.007
to sell Levi's jeans and records.

00:16:57.007 --> 00:16:58.839
And then an entrepreneur named

00:16:58.839 --> 00:17:00.812
Mickey Drexler came in and said,

00:17:00.812 --> 00:17:02.925
well, the margins on Levi's jeans

00:17:02.925 --> 00:17:03.559
are here,

00:17:03.559 --> 00:17:06.040
why don't we make Gap jeans?

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:08.440
We would get another 20% margins.

00:17:08.440 --> 00:17:10.308
Well so, for you're a retailer with

00:17:10.308 --> 00:17:11.763
stores you're not a brand.

00:17:11.763 --> 00:17:12.356
But he said no,

00:17:12.356 --> 00:17:14.580
let's vertically integrate that.

00:17:14.580 --> 00:17:16.696
And now everything from you

00:17:16.696 --> 00:17:18.076
pick it, right?

00:17:18.076 --> 00:17:19.415
From IKEA to Zara,

00:17:19.415 --> 00:17:21.007
it's all vertical now

00:17:21.007 --> 00:17:23.199
because of that margin trade.

00:17:23.199 --> 00:17:25.599
So I took those three ideas and

00:17:25.599 --> 00:17:28.383
put them together, and then I went

00:17:28.383 --> 00:17:31.263
out to pitch people on the concept,

00:17:31.263 --> 00:17:33.091
and everyone said no.

00:17:33.091 --> 00:17:35.193
And that made me really feisty.

00:17:35.193 --> 00:17:36.186
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; And

00:17:36.186 --> 00:17:38.903
also as a part of innovation which

00:17:38.903 --> 00:17:41.814
is that it's gotta be contrarian at

00:17:41.814 --> 00:17:42.696
the time.

00:17:42.696 --> 00:17:43.248
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:17:43.248 --> 00:17:45.916
you were really onto something and

00:17:45.916 --> 00:17:48.492
building this direct to consumer

00:17:48.492 --> 00:17:49.142
brand.

00:17:49.142 --> 00:17:50.244
On the surface it seems like things

00:17:50.244 --> 00:17:51.769
are going well, this is 2007, 2008.

00:17:51.769 --> 00:17:53.661
But internally, if you look under

00:17:53.661 --> 00:17:54.835
the hood of Bonobos,

00:17:54.835 --> 00:17:56.597
you were dealing with quite a bit

00:17:56.597 --> 00:17:58.621
of friction with your co-founder,

00:17:58.621 --> 00:18:00.791
Brian Staley, as you've alluded to.

00:18:00.791 --> 00:18:02.842
So can you take us back to that

00:18:02.842 --> 00:18:04.979
time in the life of Bonobos and

00:18:04.979 --> 00:18:06.613
what happened there?

00:18:06.613 --> 00:18:07.231
&gt;&gt; Well, I blamed

00:18:07.231 --> 00:18:08.045
everything on him.

00:18:08.045 --> 00:18:11.141
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; Right, it's easy in

00:18:11.141 --> 00:18:13.925
life, I think to see someone else

00:18:13.925 --> 00:18:16.817
as the problem in your life story.

00:18:16.817 --> 00:18:18.632
And then if you get to my age or

00:18:18.632 --> 00:18:21.131
beyond, 43 now, it's some point,

00:18:21.131 --> 00:18:23.253
hopefully, you realize that you

00:18:23.253 --> 00:18:25.547
are the problem in your life story.

00:18:25.547 --> 00:18:27.638
And it's a little bit debilitating

00:18:27.638 --> 00:18:28.822
to take that on, but

00:18:28.822 --> 00:18:30.984
it's also very freeing because then

00:18:30.984 --> 00:18:33.075
you don't have to stand in judgment

00:18:33.075 --> 00:18:34.623
of others in the same way.

00:18:34.623 --> 00:18:36.798
And you also have agency to fix it,

00:18:36.798 --> 00:18:38.748
if you don't need someone else

00:18:38.748 --> 00:18:39.500
to change.

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:40.881
But in 2008 and

00:18:40.881 --> 00:18:43.640
9, I was 28, 29 years old, and I

00:18:43.640 --> 00:18:46.230
had these issues with conflict that

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:47.520
I described.

00:18:47.520 --> 00:18:49.454
I wasn't able to verbalize

00:18:49.454 --> 00:18:51.220
conflict and have really

00:18:51.220 --> 00:18:53.407
difficult conversations with

00:18:53.407 --> 00:18:55.685
Brian because he was my friend.

00:18:55.685 --> 00:18:57.525
And now there's this whole school

00:18:57.525 --> 00:18:59.492
of radical candor that talks about,

00:18:59.492 --> 00:19:01.015
if you're high on caring but

00:19:01.015 --> 00:19:02.982
low on conflict, you're in a bucket

00:19:02.982 --> 00:19:04.766
that they call ruinous empathy.

00:19:04.766 --> 00:19:06.123
I was like, that's me,

00:19:06.123 --> 00:19:08.007
I'm the ruinous empathy person.

00:19:08.007 --> 00:19:09.171
And the problem with

00:19:09.171 --> 00:19:11.090
ruinous empathy is, in theory,

00:19:11.090 --> 00:19:13.008
you care about other people, but

00:19:13.008 --> 00:19:14.241
you care about them so

00:19:14.241 --> 00:19:16.160
much that you never actually tell

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:17.980
them the truth, right?

00:19:17.980 --> 00:19:20.028
Which is not that caring.

00:19:20.028 --> 00:19:22.098
And so we ended up in a place where

00:19:22.098 --> 00:19:23.709
my anger was building and

00:19:23.709 --> 00:19:25.474
building towards Brian for

00:19:25.474 --> 00:19:26.868
perceived slights.

00:19:26.868 --> 00:19:28.993
My stresses at the company as we

00:19:28.993 --> 00:19:30.465
hired more people and

00:19:30.465 --> 00:19:32.836
raise more money we're going up,

00:19:32.836 --> 00:19:35.043
and I remember having a call with

00:19:35.043 --> 00:19:37.350
one of the professors at the GSB.

00:19:38.770 --> 00:19:40.754
The only reason Bonobos exists

00:19:40.754 --> 00:19:43.270
outside of Brian's innovation, from

00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:45.634
a financial standpoint, is that two

00:19:45.634 --> 00:19:47.617
professors back the company,

00:19:47.617 --> 00:19:49.843
Joe Peterson and Andy Ratcliffe.

00:19:49.843 --> 00:19:50.595
And I called Andy one day and

00:19:50.595 --> 00:19:51.160
I sent him our P&amp;L.

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:53.910
And I was like, Brian is got,

00:19:53.910 --> 00:19:57.660
we have a major inventory problem.

00:19:57.660 --> 00:19:59.580
We designed this swimsuits thing,

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:01.116
it's not working, and Andy

00:20:01.116 --> 00:20:03.100
literally on the call was like, no,

00:20:03.100 --> 00:20:04.124
the problem on your

00:20:04.124 --> 00:20:05.650
PL is your OpEx.

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:07.377
It's your operating expenses,

00:20:07.377 --> 00:20:09.165
which is actually what Brian was,

00:20:09.165 --> 00:20:10.768
I felt criticizing me for, but

00:20:10.768 --> 00:20:12.680
it wasn't necessarily criticism it

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:14.530
was challenge, because he's like

00:20:14.530 --> 00:20:17.020
we're hiring too many people.

00:20:17.020 --> 00:20:18.702
And I was convinced that we had to

00:20:18.702 --> 00:20:20.447
bring on a great VP of engineering

00:20:20.447 --> 00:20:21.381
from Zappos, and

00:20:21.381 --> 00:20:23.203
we had to build this tech company.

00:20:23.203 --> 00:20:24.883
And Brian was, it's kind of a pants

00:20:24.883 --> 00:20:26.451
company, and I was like, well,

00:20:26.451 --> 00:20:27.907
it's a tech company that sells

00:20:27.907 --> 00:20:30.004
pants &gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; And so we had

00:20:30.004 --> 00:20:33.341
all these vectors of conflict, but

00:20:33.341 --> 00:20:36.121
we were unable to resolve it,

00:20:36.121 --> 00:20:38.345
I think in retrospect,

00:20:38.345 --> 00:20:40.583
due to my limitations.

00:20:40.583 --> 00:20:42.877
And Brian's not perfect of course,

00:20:42.877 --> 00:20:44.358
but in our relationship

00:20:44.358 --> 00:20:46.284
the friendship was ultimately

00:20:46.284 --> 00:20:47.987
sublimated to the business

00:20:47.987 --> 00:20:49.394
discussion, which is

00:20:49.394 --> 00:20:51.319
the business conversations,

00:20:51.319 --> 00:20:52.736
which is also common.

00:20:52.736 --> 00:20:55.185
So I woke up at some point dealing

00:20:55.185 --> 00:20:57.730
with a lot of depression.

00:20:57.730 --> 00:20:59.520
I really disliked Brian.

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:01.522
I wasn't so happy with myself and

00:21:01.522 --> 00:21:03.525
that led to a co-founder divorce

00:21:03.525 --> 00:21:05.314
that happened two years after

00:21:05.314 --> 00:21:07.043
the company's formation.

00:21:07.043 --> 00:21:08.668
&gt;&gt; Yeah, given the conflict you

00:21:08.668 --> 00:21:10.164
described and that leading

00:21:10.164 --> 00:21:11.660
to the co-founder split,

00:21:11.660 --> 00:21:13.480
is there any advice you would give

00:21:13.480 --> 00:21:14.910
to GSB students in terms of

00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:16.665
thinking through how not only to

00:21:16.665 --> 00:21:18.161
select a co-founder, but

00:21:18.161 --> 00:21:19.982
also safeguards to put in place in

00:21:19.982 --> 00:21:21.890
the case of co-founder divorce?

00:21:21.890 --> 00:21:23.453
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I think when it comes

00:21:23.453 --> 00:21:25.204
to selecting GSB co-founders,

00:21:25.204 --> 00:21:25.710
don't.

00:21:25.710 --> 00:21:28.935
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; But if you insist,

00:21:28.935 --> 00:21:31.287
and I do think there's something

00:21:31.287 --> 00:21:33.219
to be explored, which is,

00:21:33.219 --> 00:21:35.739
that the right co-founder for your

00:21:35.739 --> 00:21:38.007
company might be at Stanford but

00:21:38.007 --> 00:21:40.527
might not be a GSB student, someone

00:21:40.527 --> 00:21:42.879
from another school who brings an

00:21:42.879 --> 00:21:45.498
entirely complementary skill set.

00:21:45.498 --> 00:21:47.572
In very likely you're going to be

00:21:47.572 --> 00:21:49.645
the CEO of that company which is

00:21:49.645 --> 00:21:51.876
cool because CEO is a fun job, and

00:21:51.876 --> 00:21:53.949
you won't have the same kind of

00:21:53.949 --> 00:21:55.896
complimentary skill sets.

00:21:55.896 --> 00:21:58.322
If you do exist on founding

00:21:58.322 --> 00:22:01.190
a company with a Stanford, GSB

00:22:01.190 --> 00:22:04.497
classmate, and I wouldn't do it,

00:22:04.497 --> 00:22:07.712
I wouldn't do life any other way.

00:22:07.712 --> 00:22:08.762
There was no way for

00:22:08.762 --> 00:22:10.467
me to access what became my life

00:22:10.467 --> 00:22:12.305
without partnering with Brian,

00:22:12.305 --> 00:22:13.750
because he was the product

00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:16.440
innovator and the product genius.

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:18.441
It's worth really thinking about

00:22:18.441 --> 00:22:19.683
what you're going to do

00:22:19.683 --> 00:22:20.582
differently.

00:22:20.582 --> 00:22:21.961
Not at time zero, because

00:22:21.961 --> 00:22:24.030
that's pretty straightforward, but

00:22:24.030 --> 00:22:25.730
like four years later, right?

00:22:25.730 --> 00:22:28.256
So once the company has 40 people,

00:22:28.256 --> 00:22:29.812
how's it going to work?

00:22:29.812 --> 00:22:31.904
Because when everyone's in a room,

00:22:31.904 --> 00:22:33.725
it's kind of okay if you have two

00:22:33.725 --> 00:22:35.613
people running the company, but

00:22:35.613 --> 00:22:37.165
once you have 40 people,

00:22:37.165 --> 00:22:38.853
it's extremely difficult,

00:22:38.853 --> 00:22:40.015
the co-CEO thing.

00:22:40.015 --> 00:22:42.011
I love Ben Horowitz's comment on

00:22:42.011 --> 00:22:43.792
co-CEOs when he meets them,

00:22:43.792 --> 00:22:46.400
which is let me get this straight.

00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:48.756
It's more important to optimize for

00:22:48.756 --> 00:22:50.200
your egos than it is for

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:52.252
having a clear decision maker at

00:22:52.252 --> 00:22:53.392
the company, and

00:22:53.392 --> 00:22:55.446
it's a tough one to respond to.

00:22:55.446 --> 00:22:57.233
Some people do pull it off,

00:22:57.233 --> 00:22:58.943
friends of mine, Dave and

00:22:58.943 --> 00:23:00.731
Neil at Warby Parker have,

00:23:00.731 --> 00:23:02.067
it's remarkable.

00:23:02.067 --> 00:23:04.282
But I would hope that someone at

00:23:04.282 --> 00:23:06.666
the GSB will write a case about how

00:23:06.666 --> 00:23:09.318
they did it, because it's so rare.

00:23:09.318 --> 00:23:11.168
And it's so much more often

00:23:11.168 --> 00:23:13.501
the case that there is someone that

00:23:13.501 --> 00:23:15.995
leaves before the other person, and

00:23:15.995 --> 00:23:18.087
that that leaving has acrimony

00:23:18.087 --> 00:23:19.220
attached to it.

00:23:19.220 --> 00:23:20.989
And I would love to teach a case on

00:23:20.989 --> 00:23:22.761
class on co-founder divorce,

00:23:22.761 --> 00:23:24.401
because there's a bunch from

00:23:24.401 --> 00:23:26.239
the GSB, the founders of StubHub

00:23:26.239 --> 00:23:27.830
didn't speak for a decade.

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:29.504
There were founders from my class

00:23:29.504 --> 00:23:30.998
who had Adventure Together,

00:23:30.998 --> 00:23:32.014
who were married and

00:23:32.014 --> 00:23:33.339
literally got divorced.

00:23:33.339 --> 00:23:35.880
So I think it's important to,

00:23:35.880 --> 00:23:37.834
if you're going to do it,

00:23:37.834 --> 00:23:40.377
recognize that you are putting

00:23:40.377 --> 00:23:42.445
the friendship to rest.

00:23:42.445 --> 00:23:44.601
The friendship ends the day

00:23:44.601 --> 00:23:47.506
that you go into business together,

00:23:47.506 --> 00:23:49.475
because the partnership

00:23:49.475 --> 00:23:50.608
eclipses it.

00:23:50.608 --> 00:23:52.233
&gt;&gt; Those are good considerations

00:23:52.233 --> 00:23:53.237
and great advice.

00:23:53.237 --> 00:23:55.368
Also I'm sure we would love to have

00:23:55.368 --> 00:23:57.203
you and come back to the GSP and

00:23:57.203 --> 00:23:58.388
teach that class.

00:23:58.388 --> 00:24:00.357
Brian Spaly does end up going to

00:24:00.357 --> 00:24:02.478
sell Trunk Club to Nordstrom, so

00:24:02.478 --> 00:24:04.523
he does have a successful exit of

00:24:04.523 --> 00:24:05.706
his own.

00:24:05.706 --> 00:24:08.208
But, Andy, now it's 2009,

00:24:08.208 --> 00:24:10.798
you're alone at the helm of Bonobos

00:24:10.798 --> 00:24:13.303
as the sole remaining co-founder

00:24:13.303 --> 00:24:14.008
and CEO.

00:24:14.008 --> 00:24:15.647
Over the course of the next eight

00:24:15.647 --> 00:24:17.287
or nine years you are constantly

00:24:17.287 --> 00:24:19.108
innovating and trying to figure out

00:24:19.108 --> 00:24:20.271
how to grow the brand.

00:24:20.271 --> 00:24:21.153
Whether that's

00:24:21.153 --> 00:24:23.232
introducing new product categories,

00:24:23.232 --> 00:24:25.059
figuring out if you should utilize

00:24:25.059 --> 00:24:26.823
e-commerce in different ways.

00:24:26.823 --> 00:24:28.773
And then continually innovating,

00:24:28.773 --> 00:24:29.943
you end up actually,

00:24:29.943 --> 00:24:31.633
revolutionizing the brick and

00:24:31.633 --> 00:24:33.453
mortar shopping experience with

00:24:33.453 --> 00:24:34.695
Bonobos guide shops.

00:24:34.695 --> 00:24:36.376
So can you tell us a bit about how

00:24:36.376 --> 00:24:37.930
you were able to innovate and

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:39.680
constantly see gaps that others

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:40.993
couldn't at the time?

00:24:40.993 --> 00:24:43.884
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I love startups because

00:24:43.884 --> 00:24:47.219
they're a collision of fantasy and

00:24:47.219 --> 00:24:48.225
reality.

00:24:48.225 --> 00:24:50.950
And there's many genres of human

00:24:50.950 --> 00:24:53.579
work where you develop ideas and

00:24:53.579 --> 00:24:56.207
you get to get really passionate

00:24:56.207 --> 00:24:57.874
about those ideas.

00:24:57.874 --> 00:25:00.638
There are a few where those ideas

00:25:00.638 --> 00:25:02.889
are tested and really have

00:25:02.889 --> 00:25:05.958
interrogated and evaluated on a sub

00:25:05.958 --> 00:25:07.910
five year timeframe.

00:25:07.910 --> 00:25:10.028
And one of the most humbling things

00:25:10.028 --> 00:25:12.219
about being an entrepreneur is that

00:25:12.219 --> 00:25:14.410
you certainly have enough vision at

00:25:14.410 --> 00:25:16.602
the beginning to start something,

00:25:16.602 --> 00:25:18.734
if it exists, you have done that.

00:25:18.734 --> 00:25:20.713
But the false conclusion that you

00:25:20.713 --> 00:25:22.691
might reach is that you're going to

00:25:22.691 --> 00:25:24.750
be able to do that again and again.

00:25:24.750 --> 00:25:27.615
And so you might convince yourself

00:25:27.615 --> 00:25:30.685
that you're really good at vision.

00:25:30.685 --> 00:25:32.885
And the truth might be that you

00:25:32.885 --> 00:25:35.535
were really good at the beginning.

00:25:35.535 --> 00:25:37.156
And so what I learned was

00:25:37.156 --> 00:25:39.506
the innovations that were the most

00:25:39.506 --> 00:25:41.451
profound were not ones that I

00:25:41.451 --> 00:25:42.667
envisioned, but

00:25:42.667 --> 00:25:44.138
ones that I noticed.

00:25:44.138 --> 00:25:45.841
And so ust to talk about the

00:25:45.841 --> 00:25:48.086
stories, we had two fitting rooms

00:25:48.086 --> 00:25:50.333
that we fabricated in New Jersey,

00:25:50.333 --> 00:25:52.658
that were ostensibly to take around

00:25:52.658 --> 00:25:54.840
and do different pants parties.

00:25:54.840 --> 00:25:57.164
And one night we rented out a bar

00:25:57.164 --> 00:25:59.668
and we invited our best customers

00:25:59.668 --> 00:26:02.173
and it took 18 people on our team

00:26:02.173 --> 00:26:04.420
to move these fitting rooms.

00:26:04.420 --> 00:26:06.330
And it was a massive effort and

00:26:06.330 --> 00:26:07.475
we had a bar tab and

00:26:07.475 --> 00:26:09.003
everyone drank a lot and

00:26:09.003 --> 00:26:11.229
we sold like eight pairs of pants.

00:26:11.229 --> 00:26:12.003
So I was like, well,

00:26:12.003 --> 00:26:12.783
that wasn't great.

00:26:12.783 --> 00:26:14.638
The traveling fitting rooms concept

00:26:14.638 --> 00:26:16.201
is not going to be our next thing.

00:26:16.201 --> 00:26:18.064
And so they sat in our lobby

00:26:18.064 --> 00:26:19.425
collecting dust.

00:26:19.425 --> 00:26:21.322
And then when we were moving from

00:26:21.322 --> 00:26:23.010
product one to product two,

00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:25.064
from pants to button down shirts.

00:26:25.064 --> 00:26:26.918
Thinking about how to eliminate

00:26:26.918 --> 00:26:28.222
some of the we called it

00:26:28.222 --> 00:26:30.281
billowing muffin top of the shirt,

00:26:30.281 --> 00:26:31.448
which you saw none of

00:26:31.448 --> 00:26:33.246
the gentlemen who walked out.

00:26:33.246 --> 00:26:36.347
[LAUGH] It was a very tailored

00:26:36.347 --> 00:26:37.077
look.

00:26:37.077 --> 00:26:38.649
We wanted customers to come in and

00:26:38.649 --> 00:26:39.161
try it on.

00:26:39.161 --> 00:26:40.713
And so we called it the dress

00:26:40.713 --> 00:26:41.527
shirt alpha.

00:26:41.527 --> 00:26:43.353
We put it out on Twitter and

00:26:43.353 --> 00:26:45.843
Facebook, was not reviled company

00:26:45.843 --> 00:26:46.760
at the time.

00:26:46.760 --> 00:26:48.334
And people came in and

00:26:48.334 --> 00:26:49.733
they tried it on and

00:26:49.733 --> 00:26:52.270
they were like, your dress shirts

00:26:52.270 --> 00:26:54.723
suck, but can we buy some pants?

00:26:54.723 --> 00:26:55.612
And we're like,

00:26:55.612 --> 00:26:57.399
well we don't sell any pants here.

00:26:57.399 --> 00:26:58.640
And it turned into this thing of

00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:00.036
well can we just try ons and pants.

00:27:00.036 --> 00:27:02.388
And so we had a fit run and

00:27:02.388 --> 00:27:04.391
navy of every size.

00:27:04.391 --> 00:27:05.453
And then they would say,

00:27:05.453 --> 00:27:06.674
can we see the other colors?

00:27:06.674 --> 00:27:07.898
And they started transacting and

00:27:07.898 --> 00:27:09.170
it would be like, can we buy some?

00:27:09.170 --> 00:27:10.263
And it was like, well,

00:27:10.263 --> 00:27:11.576
we can just do an e-commerce

00:27:11.576 --> 00:27:12.735
transaction in person.

00:27:12.735 --> 00:27:14.885
And so at the time we were about

00:27:14.885 --> 00:27:17.037
a $10 million business and

00:27:17.037 --> 00:27:19.533
we started doing a million of sales

00:27:19.533 --> 00:27:22.127
out of our lobby unintentionally.

00:27:22.127 --> 00:27:23.781
And I remember we had recruited

00:27:23.781 --> 00:27:25.059
a merchant from J Crew.

00:27:25.059 --> 00:27:26.815
Because after Brian left I had to

00:27:26.815 --> 00:27:28.376
quickly fill the Brian sized

00:27:28.376 --> 00:27:30.067
hole at the company, which was

00:27:30.067 --> 00:27:32.216
merchandising, design, sourcing,

00:27:32.216 --> 00:27:34.052
and production, supply chain.

00:27:34.052 --> 00:27:35.910
The creative eye was hard.

00:27:35.910 --> 00:27:37.509
That was the hardest part about

00:27:37.509 --> 00:27:38.129
that year.

00:27:38.129 --> 00:27:40.090
And our VP of merchandising, a guy

00:27:40.090 --> 00:27:42.186
named Brad Andrews, who's now up at

00:27:42.186 --> 00:27:44.079
Nike, remarkable person, was I

00:27:44.079 --> 00:27:46.040
kind of like what's going on inside

00:27:46.040 --> 00:27:47.816
this little part of our lobby.

00:27:47.816 --> 00:27:49.339
And so then we said why don't we

00:27:49.339 --> 00:27:50.864
try to experiment with putting

00:27:50.864 --> 00:27:51.987
that at street level?

00:27:51.987 --> 00:27:53.339
And the unit economics

00:27:53.339 --> 00:27:55.189
were really interesting because we

00:27:55.189 --> 00:27:57.252
didn't have to stock the store and

00:27:57.252 --> 00:27:58.604
having sold watches at

00:27:58.604 --> 00:28:00.396
Marshall Fields growing up.

00:28:00.396 --> 00:28:02.128
And worked at Abercrombie and

00:28:02.128 --> 00:28:03.997
Fitch on the night shift this was

00:28:03.997 --> 00:28:06.282
20 years before the documentary, so

00:28:06.282 --> 00:28:07.331
don't judge me.

00:28:07.331 --> 00:28:08.289
But Abercrombie and

00:28:08.289 --> 00:28:09.582
Fitch sweaters when I was at

00:28:09.582 --> 00:28:11.271
Northwestern were the rage and they

00:28:11.271 --> 00:28:13.070
cost $72, I still remember this,

00:28:13.070 --> 00:28:14.377
which I couldn't afford.

00:28:14.377 --> 00:28:15.358
So I was like I will

00:28:15.358 --> 00:28:16.995
go fold sweaters at night, so

00:28:16.995 --> 00:28:18.501
I can pretend I'm rich like

00:28:18.501 --> 00:28:20.346
my classmates at Northwestern.

00:28:20.346 --> 00:28:22.257
Very sad.

00:28:22.257 --> 00:28:23.952
And folding sweaters on the night

00:28:23.952 --> 00:28:25.472
shift kind of sucks, right?

00:28:25.472 --> 00:28:26.606
Sweater after sweater,

00:28:26.606 --> 00:28:28.232
there's like the board you put in.

00:28:28.232 --> 00:28:29.977
And I remember thinking if we could

00:28:29.977 --> 00:28:31.722
build a store where no one's doing

00:28:31.722 --> 00:28:33.466
that, what could we put in instead

00:28:33.466 --> 00:28:35.042
of the inventory management.

00:28:35.042 --> 00:28:36.906
We can put in one to one service,

00:28:36.906 --> 00:28:38.701
which we turned into calling it

00:28:38.701 --> 00:28:39.468
the guides.

00:28:39.468 --> 00:28:41.607
And we opened 30 great stores, and

00:28:41.607 --> 00:28:43.470
then we opened 30 stores where

00:28:43.470 --> 00:28:44.988
we spent too much money and

00:28:44.988 --> 00:28:46.990
didn't need incremental doors in

00:28:46.990 --> 00:28:47.834
the markets.

00:28:47.834 --> 00:28:50.582
But that was the second innovation

00:28:50.582 --> 00:28:53.328
in the history of the company after

00:28:53.328 --> 00:28:55.318
the selling pants online

00:28:55.318 --> 00:28:56.464
innovation.

00:28:56.464 --> 00:28:58.267
&gt;&gt; Well these innovations helped

00:28:58.267 --> 00:28:59.375
you grow up a novice

00:28:59.375 --> 00:29:01.177
to a company that was doing over

00:29:01.177 --> 00:29:03.743
$100 million in revenue, its 2016

00:29:03.743 --> 00:29:05.825
the world's largest company comes

00:29:05.825 --> 00:29:07.228
knocking on your door.

00:29:07.228 --> 00:29:07.856
&gt;&gt; Yeah. &gt;&gt; And

00:29:07.856 --> 00:29:10.334
they end up acquiring bonobos for

00:29:10.334 --> 00:29:11.845
$310 million.

00:29:11.845 --> 00:29:13.688
Many people saw these headlines and

00:29:13.688 --> 00:29:15.593
were wondering how this acquisition

00:29:15.593 --> 00:29:17.320
might affect the Bonobos brand.

00:29:17.320 --> 00:29:18.886
So can you tell us a bit about

00:29:18.886 --> 00:29:20.180
why an acquisition and

00:29:20.180 --> 00:29:21.883
an acquisition by Walmart was

00:29:21.883 --> 00:29:23.531
the right path for Bonobos?

00:29:23.531 --> 00:29:24.307
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so

00:29:24.307 --> 00:29:26.926
we were right on the cusp of doing

00:29:26.926 --> 00:29:29.741
a deal with a private equity fund.

00:29:29.741 --> 00:29:32.540
That's really good and consumer and

00:29:32.540 --> 00:29:35.071
there's two of those globally.

00:29:35.071 --> 00:29:37.477
And that was a joke, guys.

00:29:37.477 --> 00:29:39.486
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; But it's hard when

00:29:39.486 --> 00:29:41.476
you start a company, it's hard to

00:29:41.476 --> 00:29:43.538
identify private equity investors

00:29:43.538 --> 00:29:44.991
who you want to work for.

00:29:44.991 --> 00:29:47.204
It's a big change and so we figured

00:29:47.204 --> 00:29:49.339
out a way to barely work for them,

00:29:49.339 --> 00:29:51.094
which is they were going to buy

00:29:51.094 --> 00:29:53.093
a 40% stake in the company.

00:29:53.093 --> 00:29:54.660
We were going to maintain

00:29:54.660 --> 00:29:57.300
the majority as existing investors,

00:29:57.300 --> 00:29:59.700
and they made a really good offer.

00:29:59.700 --> 00:30:01.023
And I went out to dinner at

00:30:01.023 --> 00:30:02.598
a restaurant called Auto with

00:30:02.598 --> 00:30:04.299
my CFO and our general counsel,

00:30:04.299 --> 00:30:05.685
the founder of the firm and

00:30:05.685 --> 00:30:07.327
the partner that worked on it.

00:30:07.327 --> 00:30:08.894
And they gave me the term sheet at

00:30:08.894 --> 00:30:10.288
dinner, and I walked home,

00:30:10.288 --> 00:30:11.741
which was a few blocks to where

00:30:11.741 --> 00:30:13.426
Manuel and I shared an apartment on

00:30:13.426 --> 00:30:14.196
Ninth Street.

00:30:14.196 --> 00:30:15.978
They'd given a bottle of wine as

00:30:15.978 --> 00:30:17.562
a gift and I was fumbling with

00:30:17.562 --> 00:30:18.948
the keys because I was about to

00:30:18.948 --> 00:30:21.134
show my wife a $300 million offer.

00:30:21.134 --> 00:30:23.377
And as I was fumbling I dropped

00:30:23.377 --> 00:30:25.710
the offer and the bottle of wine

00:30:25.710 --> 00:30:27.073
at the same time.

00:30:27.073 --> 00:30:29.871
And there was this guy named Newton

00:30:29.871 --> 00:30:32.572
who said that two things will drop

00:30:32.572 --> 00:30:35.371
at the same rate regardless of size

00:30:35.371 --> 00:30:38.072
and so the bottle of wine exploded

00:30:38.072 --> 00:30:39.247
on the offer.

00:30:39.247 --> 00:30:40.673
And so I walked in with

00:30:40.673 --> 00:30:42.820
an offer that was dripping red.

00:30:42.820 --> 00:30:43.968
And I was like, Manuel,

00:30:43.968 --> 00:30:45.233
I've got the blood money.

00:30:45.233 --> 00:30:50.547
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; We're selling out.

00:30:50.547 --> 00:30:52.732
And during that process and

00:30:52.732 --> 00:30:55.202
in truth I really did like these

00:30:55.202 --> 00:30:57.968
people and I felt like they got it.

00:30:57.968 --> 00:30:59.676
They had this thing they needed

00:30:59.676 --> 00:31:01.513
to do, which is they needed to put

00:31:01.513 --> 00:31:03.286
their capital ahead of everyone

00:31:03.286 --> 00:31:05.204
else's in the preference stack.

00:31:05.204 --> 00:31:06.288
And I was like,

00:31:06.288 --> 00:31:08.465
you guys just got to the party.

00:31:08.465 --> 00:31:10.215
Why are you in the first in line?

00:31:10.215 --> 00:31:11.862
And so we were messing around for

00:31:11.862 --> 00:31:12.838
weeks and weeks and

00:31:12.838 --> 00:31:14.424
then I heard from someone hey,

00:31:14.424 --> 00:31:16.083
you should talk to Mark Laurie.

00:31:16.083 --> 00:31:17.928
Mark had founded diapers.com and

00:31:17.928 --> 00:31:18.945
sold it to Amazon,

00:31:18.945 --> 00:31:20.344
then he founded something

00:31:20.344 --> 00:31:21.815
called jet and it was wild.

00:31:21.815 --> 00:31:23.674
He came out of Amazon and was like,

00:31:23.674 --> 00:31:25.159
we're going to beat Amazon.

00:31:25.159 --> 00:31:27.245
And the rumor was that he was

00:31:27.245 --> 00:31:30.154
burning 20 million a month in cash.

00:31:30.154 --> 00:31:31.810
And then he sold the company to

00:31:31.810 --> 00:31:33.600
Walmart for 3 billion which was

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:35.400
the only buyer on planet earth.

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:37.448
So who does that?.

00:31:37.448 --> 00:31:39.054
And I walked into meet Mark

00:31:39.054 --> 00:31:40.076
in Hoboken and he

00:31:40.076 --> 00:31:42.342
was wearing a Vineyard Vines shirt.

00:31:42.342 --> 00:31:44.421
And I was like, I didn't walk in

00:31:44.421 --> 00:31:46.737
here in an f'ing Amazon t-shirt.

00:31:46.737 --> 00:31:48.415
He didn't care that much about

00:31:48.415 --> 00:31:49.384
the meeting, but

00:31:49.384 --> 00:31:51.192
then we sat down and we mind melded

00:31:51.192 --> 00:31:53.000
on this concept that the future of

00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:54.550
commerce might look a little

00:31:54.550 --> 00:31:56.246
bit like the history of video.

00:31:56.246 --> 00:31:57.739
Which is that initially the big

00:31:57.739 --> 00:31:59.290
platforms were streaming other

00:31:59.290 --> 00:32:00.898
people's movies and shows, and

00:32:00.898 --> 00:32:02.277
eventually that was a race to

00:32:02.277 --> 00:32:03.828
the bottom on price, and so they

00:32:03.828 --> 00:32:05.719
would need to develop their own.

00:32:05.719 --> 00:32:07.309
Content to win.

00:32:07.309 --> 00:32:09.130
And when Netflix launched House of

00:32:09.130 --> 00:32:10.949
Cards and Orange is the New Black,

00:32:10.949 --> 00:32:12.452
if you go back and look at it,

00:32:12.452 --> 00:32:13.895
their share price took a hit

00:32:13.895 --> 00:32:15.275
because Wall Street was like,

00:32:15.275 --> 00:32:16.779
what are you trying to do to,

00:32:16.779 --> 00:32:17.508
become HBO?

00:32:17.508 --> 00:32:19.298
And in fact, that turned out

00:32:19.298 --> 00:32:20.645
to be an astute move.

00:32:20.645 --> 00:32:23.076
So Mark and I that I would come in

00:32:23.076 --> 00:32:25.066
and run this new digital private

00:32:25.066 --> 00:32:26.626
brand group at Walmart.

00:32:26.626 --> 00:32:28.618
And he said you gotta meet Doug.

00:32:28.618 --> 00:32:31.336
And I went down to Soho one night

00:32:31.336 --> 00:32:33.846
and met this Doug McMillon,

00:32:33.846 --> 00:32:36.358
unassuming guy, for dinner

00:32:36.358 --> 00:32:39.509
with Mark, and he, how do I put it?

00:32:39.509 --> 00:32:41.167
I came out of the dinner being

00:32:41.167 --> 00:32:43.032
like, it would be a privilege to

00:32:43.032 --> 00:32:44.630
work for this individual.

00:32:44.630 --> 00:32:46.203
I fell in professional love, and

00:32:46.203 --> 00:32:47.946
I'm still in professional love with

00:32:47.946 --> 00:32:48.740
Doug McMillon.

00:32:48.740 --> 00:32:50.911
He's the best leader I've ever seen

00:32:50.911 --> 00:32:52.943
in action, and we should get him up

00:32:52.943 --> 00:32:54.636
in this chair at some point.

00:32:54.636 --> 00:32:55.759
And I was like, wow,

00:32:55.759 --> 00:32:57.509
it would be really cool to see this

00:32:57.509 --> 00:32:59.260
Walmart-Amazon battle play out

00:32:59.260 --> 00:33:00.821
with not a front row seat, but

00:33:00.821 --> 00:33:02.279
a second or third row seat.

00:33:02.279 --> 00:33:04.169
&gt;&gt; Well, it's quite impressive that

00:33:04.169 --> 00:33:05.807
you became a part of the digital

00:33:05.807 --> 00:33:07.635
transformation of the Fortune one

00:33:07.635 --> 00:33:08.208
company.

00:33:08.208 --> 00:33:10.291
And this obviously was such a big

00:33:10.291 --> 00:33:12.538
milestone in the life of bonobos.

00:33:12.538 --> 00:33:14.620
And all throughout you growing and

00:33:14.620 --> 00:33:16.918
scaling the company, you internally

00:33:16.918 --> 00:33:18.511
are dealing with so much.

00:33:18.511 --> 00:33:19.926
So I want to pivot now and

00:33:19.926 --> 00:33:21.714
talk a bit about your bipolar

00:33:21.714 --> 00:33:23.139
disorder diagnosis.

00:33:23.139 --> 00:33:24.723
Tell us more about not only

00:33:24.723 --> 00:33:26.596
the diagnosis in college, but

00:33:26.596 --> 00:33:28.404
also your initial reaction.

00:33:28.404 --> 00:33:30.152
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I was diagnosed

00:33:30.152 --> 00:33:32.322
when I was a senior in college.

00:33:32.322 --> 00:33:34.746
I had a few weeks where I kind of

00:33:34.746 --> 00:33:37.784
took my partying to the next level.

00:33:37.784 --> 00:33:39.198
I was already drinking a lot,

00:33:39.198 --> 00:33:40.449
smoking a little bit pot.

00:33:40.449 --> 00:33:42.581
But I moved into a bit of ecstasy

00:33:42.581 --> 00:33:44.877
and I had my first experience with

00:33:44.877 --> 00:33:47.583
psilocybin mushrooms, which for me,

00:33:47.583 --> 00:33:49.145
luckily was the last.

00:33:49.145 --> 00:33:50.478
A few weeks later,

00:33:50.478 --> 00:33:52.893
it was the turn of the millennium,

00:33:52.893 --> 00:33:55.156
so it was 1999 to 2000.

00:33:55.156 --> 00:33:57.053
And it was a very exciting time,

00:33:57.053 --> 00:33:58.669
because there was this Y2K

00:33:58.669 --> 00:34:00.567
narrative about how the digital

00:34:00.567 --> 00:34:02.194
world was going to melt down.

00:34:02.194 --> 00:34:03.651
And then there was this like,

00:34:03.651 --> 00:34:05.409
what does the new millennium mean?

00:34:05.409 --> 00:34:07.517
And so as I was using substances,

00:34:07.517 --> 00:34:09.324
and had really fallen in love

00:34:09.324 --> 00:34:11.207
in a really meaningful way for

00:34:11.207 --> 00:34:12.883
the first time in my life.

00:34:12.883 --> 00:34:15.251
I was watching one night a special

00:34:15.251 --> 00:34:17.704
on people who thought that this was

00:34:17.704 --> 00:34:19.819
going to be the end of the world,

00:34:19.819 --> 00:34:21.849
or that the Messiah was going to

00:34:21.849 --> 00:34:22.536
arrive.

00:34:22.536 --> 00:34:25.324
And as my mood escalated, at some

00:34:25.324 --> 00:34:28.318
point, I realized I was arriving.

00:34:28.318 --> 00:34:29.406
I thought that I was

00:34:29.406 --> 00:34:30.566
the second coming.

00:34:30.566 --> 00:34:33.415
And part of what is so humiliating

00:34:33.415 --> 00:34:36.162
about mania is that we have these

00:34:36.162 --> 00:34:39.130
messianic grandiose delusions.

00:34:39.130 --> 00:34:40.594
And when you come out of it,

00:34:40.594 --> 00:34:42.826
you're like everyone now knows that

00:34:42.826 --> 00:34:44.819
I have this underlying God complex.

00:34:44.819 --> 00:34:46.957
And so it took multiple years later

00:34:46.957 --> 00:34:49.022
of therapy for my psychiatrist,

00:34:49.022 --> 00:34:51.013
who I affectionately refer to in

00:34:51.013 --> 00:34:51.971
the book as Dr.

00:34:51.971 --> 00:34:54.034
Z, to make me aware that everyone

00:34:54.034 --> 00:34:55.826
actually has a God complex.

00:34:55.826 --> 00:34:58.020
And the God complex begins in

00:34:58.020 --> 00:35:00.855
infancy, because when you scream,

00:35:00.855 --> 00:35:03.323
you get milk, and when you cry,

00:35:03.323 --> 00:35:04.889
you get comforted.

00:35:04.889 --> 00:35:05.588
This is for

00:35:05.588 --> 00:35:07.608
the vast majority of infants,

00:35:07.608 --> 00:35:09.167
not everyone, sadly.

00:35:09.167 --> 00:35:11.567
And so we begin our lives as all

00:35:11.567 --> 00:35:13.103
powerful beings,

00:35:13.103 --> 00:35:15.695
and then we slowly part with that

00:35:15.695 --> 00:35:17.525
reality year by year.

00:35:17.525 --> 00:35:19.517
At the same time as Disney and

00:35:19.517 --> 00:35:21.675
Harry Potter incept us with the

00:35:21.675 --> 00:35:24.082
possibility that we may be orphans

00:35:24.082 --> 00:35:26.661
who turned out to have superpowers.

00:35:26.661 --> 00:35:29.165
Which is to say that our popular

00:35:29.165 --> 00:35:31.092
culture is built around

00:35:31.092 --> 00:35:33.405
the struggling childhood to

00:35:33.405 --> 00:35:35.052
superpower thing.

00:35:35.052 --> 00:35:37.834
And so most of us discard the idea

00:35:37.834 --> 00:35:40.204
that we have superpowers,

00:35:40.204 --> 00:35:42.479
should they ever come up.

00:35:42.479 --> 00:35:44.332
When you're psychotic, and one of

00:35:44.332 --> 00:35:45.876
the things that might help you

00:35:45.876 --> 00:35:47.730
understand a person in psychosis,

00:35:47.730 --> 00:35:49.521
is that you don't have the ability

00:35:49.521 --> 00:35:51.027
to disregard that thought.

00:35:51.027 --> 00:35:52.852
The irrational thoughts that you

00:35:52.852 --> 00:35:54.740
might have at any point in the day,

00:35:54.740 --> 00:35:56.499
the plane hit some turbulence,

00:35:56.499 --> 00:35:58.209
we're all going to die, right?

00:35:58.209 --> 00:36:00.953
Or that deer, maybe that's my

00:36:00.953 --> 00:36:03.593
daughter who passed on, like

00:36:03.593 --> 00:36:06.771
the movie with Frances McDormand.

00:36:06.771 --> 00:36:08.046
Those kinds of thoughts

00:36:08.046 --> 00:36:09.958
are fantastical, and they're not

00:36:09.958 --> 00:36:11.816
discarded by a psychotic person.

00:36:11.816 --> 00:36:13.992
And so I spent a week as Jesus 2.0,

00:36:13.992 --> 00:36:15.195
and it was amazing.

00:36:15.195 --> 00:36:19.476
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; It was amazing.

00:36:19.476 --> 00:36:22.068
Being the Messiah when manic is so

00:36:22.068 --> 00:36:25.031
fun, if you actually believe that's

00:36:25.031 --> 00:36:25.594
true.

00:36:25.594 --> 00:36:27.210
And then it turns out that you're

00:36:27.210 --> 00:36:28.884
actually the biggest liability on

00:36:28.884 --> 00:36:30.683
the planet for everyone around you.

00:36:30.683 --> 00:36:33.999
So I stopped eating.

00:36:33.999 --> 00:36:35.938
I stopped drinking anything,

00:36:35.938 --> 00:36:37.135
including water.

00:36:37.135 --> 00:36:38.911
I stayed up for, we think,

00:36:38.911 --> 00:36:40.310
four nights in a row.

00:36:40.310 --> 00:36:42.047
And somehow through,

00:36:42.047 --> 00:36:44.882
my friends figuring it out, calling

00:36:44.882 --> 00:36:47.443
my parents, my parents bringing

00:36:47.443 --> 00:36:50.095
me home, me lecturing my dad on all

00:36:50.095 --> 00:36:51.382
kinds of stuff.

00:36:51.382 --> 00:36:52.568
My whole theory was that I was

00:36:52.568 --> 00:36:53.902
the Messiah and my message, and

00:36:53.902 --> 00:36:55.336
some of you may like this message,

00:36:55.336 --> 00:36:56.682
is that God is actually a woman.

00:36:56.682 --> 00:36:59.700
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; It's like why did

00:36:59.700 --> 00:37:02.371
men get all this credit throughout

00:37:02.371 --> 00:37:04.774
history when women make life.

00:37:04.774 --> 00:37:06.606
And so I was yelling at my dad

00:37:06.606 --> 00:37:08.518
about this, and I was like,

00:37:08.518 --> 00:37:10.690
all your heroes are white guys.

00:37:10.690 --> 00:37:12.150
And my dad was a master,

00:37:12.150 --> 00:37:14.194
he just went full Socrates on me,

00:37:14.194 --> 00:37:16.099
and was like mm-hm, mm-hm.

00:37:16.099 --> 00:37:18.266
And then they called over my aunt,

00:37:18.266 --> 00:37:20.134
the one who delivered me, and

00:37:20.134 --> 00:37:22.302
my uncle who are both physicians,

00:37:22.302 --> 00:37:23.647
and they sat on either

00:37:23.647 --> 00:37:25.587
side of me in the back of a car and

00:37:25.587 --> 00:37:26.960
took me to hospital.

00:37:26.960 --> 00:37:28.709
And that happened to be a hospital

00:37:28.709 --> 00:37:30.394
where I had worked growing up and

00:37:30.394 --> 00:37:32.205
the hospital where my mom worked,

00:37:32.205 --> 00:37:33.392
which just compounded

00:37:33.392 --> 00:37:35.027
the humiliation afterwards.

00:37:35.027 --> 00:37:36.775
And so when the diagnosing

00:37:36.775 --> 00:37:37.968
physician said,

00:37:37.968 --> 00:37:40.353
you have bipolar disorder type 1,

00:37:40.353 --> 00:37:42.896
I remember thinking, no, I don't,

00:37:42.896 --> 00:37:44.337
like, what is that?

00:37:44.337 --> 00:37:45.027
Two weeks ago,

00:37:45.027 --> 00:37:46.514
I was having fun with my friends,

00:37:46.514 --> 00:37:48.120
I don't even know what that means.

00:37:48.120 --> 00:37:50.260
And then I went home and I did some

00:37:50.260 --> 00:37:52.084
learning and found out that

00:37:52.084 --> 00:37:54.541
the suicide attempt rate is 60%,

00:37:54.541 --> 00:37:56.693
and the suicide rate is 20%.

00:37:56.693 --> 00:37:58.723
So when you're 20, and you're like,

00:37:58.723 --> 00:38:00.289
well, there's statistically

00:38:00.289 --> 00:38:01.043
a 1 in 5 chance

00:38:01.043 --> 00:38:02.382
I'm going to end my own life.

00:38:02.382 --> 00:38:04.264
And by the way, this whole mania

00:38:04.264 --> 00:38:06.216
thing can come back at any point,

00:38:06.216 --> 00:38:08.098
could be two weeks or ten years,

00:38:08.098 --> 00:38:09.980
and you might do anything in that

00:38:09.980 --> 00:38:11.808
state, like cut off your ear.

00:38:11.808 --> 00:38:13.165
It's very hard to take that

00:38:13.165 --> 00:38:15.172
diagnosis on as a 20-year-old, and

00:38:15.172 --> 00:38:16.824
it's most frequently diagnosed

00:38:16.824 --> 00:38:18.013
between 18 and 25.

00:38:18.013 --> 00:38:18.772
And so back to

00:38:18.772 --> 00:38:20.914
the family norms of communication,

00:38:20.914 --> 00:38:22.710
very different families, but

00:38:22.710 --> 00:38:24.712
one thing that cultures did have in

00:38:24.712 --> 00:38:26.647
common was an abject desire to not

00:38:26.647 --> 00:38:28.321
speak about mental health.

00:38:28.321 --> 00:38:30.486
And so my diagnosis became a little

00:38:30.486 --> 00:38:32.652
bit like fight club, which is, we

00:38:32.652 --> 00:38:35.129
don't talk about fight club, right?

00:38:35.129 --> 00:38:36.528
We just buried it, and

00:38:36.528 --> 00:38:38.156
my friends buried it too.

00:38:38.156 --> 00:38:39.922
And so I internalized that as

00:38:39.922 --> 00:38:41.909
an unspeakable shame, right,

00:38:41.909 --> 00:38:43.601
because what is shame, but

00:38:43.601 --> 00:38:45.009
what is unspeakable?

00:38:45.009 --> 00:38:46.610
And the amazing thing about

00:38:46.610 --> 00:38:48.002
the human mind is you can

00:38:48.002 --> 00:38:50.236
actually bury this trauma yourself.

00:38:50.236 --> 00:38:52.244
So I became unaware that these

00:38:52.244 --> 00:38:54.653
events had transpired, and then in

00:38:54.653 --> 00:38:57.064
occasion would remember this thing

00:38:57.064 --> 00:38:59.247
that happened to me in college.

00:38:59.247 --> 00:39:00.739
I wasn't even aware.

00:39:00.739 --> 00:39:02.355
And then I would remember and

00:39:02.355 --> 00:39:04.247
have this rising panic and fear.

00:39:04.247 --> 00:39:05.584
Wait, what does that mean,

00:39:05.584 --> 00:39:06.255
is that true?

00:39:06.255 --> 00:39:07.955
And then push it back down.

00:39:07.955 --> 00:39:11.669
And I lived that way for 16 years.

00:39:11.669 --> 00:39:13.734
And I lived asymptomatic for

00:39:13.734 --> 00:39:16.142
the first eight, which lulled us

00:39:16.142 --> 00:39:17.949
collectively as a family

00:39:17.949 --> 00:39:20.357
into a feeling that perhaps it had

00:39:20.357 --> 00:39:22.693
been an inaccurate diagnosis.

00:39:22.693 --> 00:39:24.556
And then in bonobos years,

00:39:24.556 --> 00:39:26.419
depression kicked in, and

00:39:26.419 --> 00:39:28.689
ultimately it came raging back.

00:39:28.689 --> 00:39:30.101
&gt;&gt; Wo, and you mentioned it came

00:39:30.101 --> 00:39:30.783
raging back.

00:39:30.783 --> 00:39:31.868
Can you tell us a bit about

00:39:31.868 --> 00:39:33.109
the episode that I described

00:39:33.109 --> 00:39:34.660
at the beginning of the interview,

00:39:34.660 --> 00:39:35.695
where you find yourself

00:39:35.695 --> 00:39:36.746
in Bellevue for a week?

00:39:36.746 --> 00:39:37.290
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so

00:39:37.290 --> 00:39:39.194
one of the most pernicious things

00:39:39.194 --> 00:39:40.758
about mania is that it can be

00:39:40.758 --> 00:39:42.866
triggered by positive life events,

00:39:42.866 --> 00:39:44.906
having a child, getting married,

00:39:44.906 --> 00:39:46.887
let's say selling your company.

00:39:46.887 --> 00:39:48.908
In my case, it was falling in love

00:39:48.908 --> 00:39:49.920
with Manuela and

00:39:49.920 --> 00:39:51.872
coming to the decision that I was

00:39:51.872 --> 00:39:53.477
going to ask her to marry me.

00:39:53.477 --> 00:39:55.074
And I did it in a very romantic

00:39:55.074 --> 00:39:56.472
way, when while manic,

00:39:56.472 --> 00:39:58.403
I showed her a picture of the ring,

00:39:58.403 --> 00:40:00.282
which is a great reveal, right?

00:40:00.282 --> 00:40:00.864
I was so

00:40:00.864 --> 00:40:03.679
excited during that time in my life

00:40:03.679 --> 00:40:06.495
that I kind of replicated the going

00:40:06.495 --> 00:40:08.730
off the rails of Going out a lot,

00:40:08.730 --> 00:40:11.106
drinking a lot, not sleeping much.

00:40:11.106 --> 00:40:11.885
Sleep quality

00:40:11.885 --> 00:40:13.771
is poor if you're drinking a lot.

00:40:13.771 --> 00:40:14.540
She left,

00:40:14.540 --> 00:40:16.589
she was working and spending

00:40:16.589 --> 00:40:18.905
time in Beijing at that moment.

00:40:18.905 --> 00:40:20.556
And while she was out I was

00:40:20.556 --> 00:40:22.289
just going off the rails,

00:40:22.289 --> 00:40:24.493
burning the candle at both ends,

00:40:24.493 --> 00:40:26.933
which wasn't terribly uncommon for

00:40:26.933 --> 00:40:28.604
me but it was more acute.

00:40:28.604 --> 00:40:31.558
And when she came back, I was off.

00:40:31.558 --> 00:40:33.852
But she wasn't quite sure how to

00:40:33.852 --> 00:40:35.722
process it because while I

00:40:35.722 --> 00:40:37.847
had told her that I'd had this

00:40:37.847 --> 00:40:39.463
incident in college,

00:40:39.463 --> 00:40:41.588
I told her what also was true,

00:40:41.588 --> 00:40:44.052
which was that bipolar disorder is

00:40:44.052 --> 00:40:46.347
a differential diagnosis, and

00:40:46.347 --> 00:40:48.812
people aren't always definitely

00:40:48.812 --> 00:40:51.140
sure that that's what it is.

00:40:51.140 --> 00:40:52.692
It's both over-diagnosed and

00:40:52.692 --> 00:40:54.450
under-diagnosed.

00:40:54.450 --> 00:40:56.165
And I had gone to a few mental

00:40:56.165 --> 00:40:58.255
health professionals in New York

00:40:58.255 --> 00:41:00.045
when I was dealing with acute

00:41:00.045 --> 00:41:00.951
depression.

00:41:00.951 --> 00:41:02.567
And most people were like,

00:41:02.567 --> 00:41:04.675
the company that you're running,

00:41:04.675 --> 00:41:06.783
that's a really stressful thing,

00:41:06.783 --> 00:41:08.751
and you don't strike me as having

00:41:08.751 --> 00:41:09.673
this illness.

00:41:09.673 --> 00:41:11.155
And so I had a few people who kind

00:41:11.155 --> 00:41:12.010
of dismissed it.

00:41:13.600 --> 00:41:15.779
So she wasn't really prepared, and

00:41:15.779 --> 00:41:17.350
it got bad that night.

00:41:17.350 --> 00:41:18.755
I was laying in bed,

00:41:18.755 --> 00:41:20.586
I was howling at the moon.

00:41:20.586 --> 00:41:23.639
I was playing Tupac really loud,

00:41:23.639 --> 00:41:26.153
and music sounded so good.

00:41:26.153 --> 00:41:27.980
And she didn't know what to do, so

00:41:27.980 --> 00:41:29.051
she called her mom,

00:41:29.051 --> 00:41:30.061
her mom came over.

00:41:30.061 --> 00:41:32.135
Her mom lived in New York.

00:41:32.135 --> 00:41:33.585
And they were trying to kind of

00:41:33.585 --> 00:41:34.282
comfort me and

00:41:34.282 --> 00:41:36.030
manage the situation.

00:41:36.030 --> 00:41:37.680
And I got up with a start to go to

00:41:37.680 --> 00:41:39.070
the bathroom.

00:41:39.070 --> 00:41:40.995
And we lived in a little garden

00:41:40.995 --> 00:41:42.458
apartment where there

00:41:42.458 --> 00:41:44.152
was kind of a low ceiling and

00:41:44.152 --> 00:41:46.077
I hit my head on the ceiling and

00:41:46.077 --> 00:41:48.387
cracked open and was bleeding, and

00:41:48.387 --> 00:41:49.710
it hurt really bad.

00:41:49.710 --> 00:41:50.901
Plus with the mania,

00:41:50.901 --> 00:41:52.230
you feel more acutely.

00:41:53.600 --> 00:41:54.847
And we'd had this fight

00:41:54.847 --> 00:41:56.430
with our landlord.

00:41:56.430 --> 00:41:59.419
And there was a heating grate that

00:41:59.419 --> 00:42:02.513
had rats that could come out of it,

00:42:02.513 --> 00:42:05.560
and I hated our landlord.

00:42:05.560 --> 00:42:07.408
And so I did something I had wanted

00:42:07.408 --> 00:42:08.926
to do for a long time which is

00:42:08.926 --> 00:42:10.713
I rip the grade off and threw it.

00:42:10.713 --> 00:42:13.696
And then I punched a large glass

00:42:13.696 --> 00:42:15.188
window pane and

00:42:15.188 --> 00:42:18.630
opened up both of my knuckles here,

00:42:18.630 --> 00:42:21.497
and I can still see the scars as

00:42:21.497 --> 00:42:24.134
kind of I take it as a healthy

00:42:24.134 --> 00:42:27.269
reminder of what's possible.

00:42:27.269 --> 00:42:30.251
And then Manuela came to help me,

00:42:30.251 --> 00:42:33.126
and I remember seeing the fear in

00:42:33.126 --> 00:42:34.094
her eyes.

00:42:34.094 --> 00:42:36.650
And my immediate thought was this

00:42:36.650 --> 00:42:38.291
is sort of weird, but

00:42:38.291 --> 00:42:40.664
I thought she was the alien from

00:42:40.664 --> 00:42:42.762
Ex Machina because she kind of is

00:42:42.762 --> 00:42:45.591
stunningly beautiful, and smart,

00:42:45.591 --> 00:42:48.331
and a little bit robotics sometimes

00:42:48.331 --> 00:42:50.978
with her intelligence and logic,

00:42:50.978 --> 00:42:52.554
and so I struck her.

00:42:52.554 --> 00:42:54.550
And then her mom came out.

00:42:54.550 --> 00:42:55.902
And the felony charge was that I

00:42:55.902 --> 00:42:57.254
pushed her mom to the ground and

00:42:57.254 --> 00:42:57.880
I kicked her.

00:42:59.360 --> 00:43:02.966
And they were extremely brave.

00:43:02.966 --> 00:43:04.311
Manuela got her mom to

00:43:04.311 --> 00:43:06.784
the bathroom, her mom called 911.

00:43:07.920 --> 00:43:09.752
Eight New York police officers

00:43:09.752 --> 00:43:12.610
showed up within about two minutes.

00:43:12.610 --> 00:43:14.578
They pinned me to the ground,

00:43:14.578 --> 00:43:16.230
cuffed me, and took me to

00:43:16.230 --> 00:43:18.514
the psychiatric emergency room at

00:43:18.514 --> 00:43:20.560
Bellevue, where I spent a week

00:43:20.560 --> 00:43:22.625
unaware of what had happened.

00:43:22.625 --> 00:43:24.571
And as I came down from it,

00:43:24.571 --> 00:43:26.605
Manuela came to visit every

00:43:26.605 --> 00:43:28.639
day which was incredible,

00:43:28.639 --> 00:43:31.215
I noticed that she had a black eye.

00:43:31.215 --> 00:43:33.144
And it was about day five or

00:43:33.144 --> 00:43:35.522
six and I just said did I hit you?

00:43:35.522 --> 00:43:38.410
And she said you're kind of a wuss

00:43:38.410 --> 00:43:40.272
and it didn't hurt.

00:43:40.272 --> 00:43:43.618
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; And then it was

00:43:43.618 --> 00:43:46.210
a noose to the sky of shame to know

00:43:46.210 --> 00:43:48.233
that that had happened.

00:43:48.233 --> 00:43:49.942
&gt;&gt; Wow, after that episode,

00:43:49.942 --> 00:43:51.652
how did you think about your

00:43:51.652 --> 00:43:53.586
bipolar disorder diagnosis,

00:43:53.586 --> 00:43:55.590
and what do you do today to help to

00:43:55.590 --> 00:43:57.391
keep it under control of it?

00:43:57.391 --> 00:43:58.263
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so

00:43:58.263 --> 00:44:00.880
coming out of the hospital,

00:44:00.880 --> 00:44:04.043
walking into handcuffs, the next

00:44:04.043 --> 00:44:07.324
six months were like a hell escape.

00:44:07.324 --> 00:44:09.750
And it was, was Manuela going to

00:44:09.750 --> 00:44:12.078
leave me, was I going to have to

00:44:12.078 --> 00:44:13.921
step down from my job,

00:44:13.921 --> 00:44:16.650
was there going to be PR about it?

00:44:16.650 --> 00:44:18.138
I remember being in

00:44:18.138 --> 00:44:20.742
the Fourth Precinct in New York.

00:44:20.742 --> 00:44:22.103
A place I never thought I'd be,

00:44:22.103 --> 00:44:23.415
it was across the street from my

00:44:23.415 --> 00:44:24.425
favorite restaurant,

00:44:24.425 --> 00:44:26.250
this Italian restaurant called.

00:44:26.250 --> 00:44:27.735
I never even noticed that there,

00:44:27.735 --> 00:44:28.954
this isn't sound funny but

00:44:28.954 --> 00:44:30.386
a cute police department across

00:44:30.386 --> 00:44:31.410
the street.

00:44:31.410 --> 00:44:33.022
And now I'm in the cell there,

00:44:33.022 --> 00:44:33.767
the arresting

00:44:33.767 --> 00:44:35.930
officer is the one booking me.

00:44:35.930 --> 00:44:37.621
He took my mug shots and

00:44:37.621 --> 00:44:40.291
I said, Officer are these mug shots

00:44:40.291 --> 00:44:42.270
going to be online?

00:44:42.270 --> 00:44:44.129
And he was like, yeah, but dude,

00:44:44.129 --> 00:44:46.054
you're not the founder of Google,

00:44:46.054 --> 00:44:46.965
you sell pants.

00:44:46.965 --> 00:44:49.542
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; And that was true,

00:44:49.542 --> 00:44:50.729
that was true, right?

00:44:50.729 --> 00:44:52.608
If I had had a higher public

00:44:52.608 --> 00:44:55.086
profile there probably would have

00:44:55.086 --> 00:44:57.136
been press because of the mug

00:44:57.136 --> 00:44:57.743
shots.

00:44:57.743 --> 00:44:59.238
It was under my legal name

00:44:59.238 --> 00:45:01.870
Andrew Dan, which is not a thing.

00:45:01.870 --> 00:45:03.507
And I was in and out of court, and

00:45:03.507 --> 00:45:05.026
there are court reporters but

00:45:05.026 --> 00:45:05.694
it wasn't,

00:45:05.694 --> 00:45:07.470
it never happened on the PR front.

00:45:09.250 --> 00:45:10.650
I was supported by the board.

00:45:11.770 --> 00:45:13.445
Ben Wallace stayed with me but

00:45:13.445 --> 00:45:14.450
with condition,

00:45:14.450 --> 00:45:16.460
which is that I took my medication,

00:45:16.460 --> 00:45:18.135
and saw a doctor, and I became

00:45:18.135 --> 00:45:20.149
religious about staying healthy.

00:45:20.149 --> 00:45:21.891
I started seeing a psychiatrist in

00:45:21.891 --> 00:45:23.512
lower Manhattan twice a week for

00:45:23.512 --> 00:45:25.100
45 minutes.

00:45:25.100 --> 00:45:26.400
I did whatever he said,

00:45:26.400 --> 00:45:28.545
medication-wise, and that included

00:45:28.545 --> 00:45:30.235
six months of experimentation

00:45:30.235 --> 00:45:32.060
where I felt suicidal everyday.

00:45:33.170 --> 00:45:35.509
And I also knew, because after the

00:45:35.509 --> 00:45:37.775
big highs is a usually a long low.

00:45:37.775 --> 00:45:39.309
The big high is fast,

00:45:39.309 --> 00:45:41.526
it's like a week, the low that

00:45:41.526 --> 00:45:43.742
follows it can be a half a year or

00:45:43.742 --> 00:45:45.875
years depending on the kind of

00:45:45.875 --> 00:45:48.545
treatment that you have available.

00:45:48.545 --> 00:45:49.193
And I had great

00:45:49.193 --> 00:45:50.275
treatment available,

00:45:50.275 --> 00:45:51.789
which is a fundamental injustice

00:45:51.789 --> 00:45:53.356
that we have in our society, which

00:45:53.356 --> 00:45:54.979
is that mental health is reimbursed

00:45:54.979 --> 00:45:56.491
at a very low rate, and it's hard

00:45:56.491 --> 00:45:58.610
to find the right provider.

00:45:58.610 --> 00:46:01.252
But I got through that, and so

00:46:01.252 --> 00:46:04.107
now I have a pretty robust system

00:46:04.107 --> 00:46:06.290
of medication.

00:46:06.290 --> 00:46:08.100
I still see that doctor twice

00:46:08.100 --> 00:46:10.288
a week, and sleep is so critical,

00:46:10.288 --> 00:46:12.024
sleep is foundational, so

00:46:12.024 --> 00:46:14.136
I have a sleep report that I send,

00:46:14.136 --> 00:46:16.174
screenshot of a Fitbit report,

00:46:16.174 --> 00:46:17.456
a WhatsApp group and

00:46:17.456 --> 00:46:19.520
it goes every morning.

00:46:19.520 --> 00:46:21.127
And so everyone knew yesterday

00:46:21.127 --> 00:46:22.671
which is a little bit annoying

00:46:22.671 --> 00:46:24.214
because I know my mom worries about

00:46:24.214 --> 00:46:26.004
it still, that night before last I

00:46:26.004 --> 00:46:27.880
slept four hours and 33 minutes.

00:46:28.910 --> 00:46:30.068
And everyone knew I was coming

00:46:30.068 --> 00:46:30.807
to give this talk,

00:46:30.807 --> 00:46:32.140
which I was excited about.

00:46:32.140 --> 00:46:33.365
And so last night was a high

00:46:33.365 --> 00:46:35.360
pressure sleep night.

00:46:35.360 --> 00:46:36.462
So I was getting dinner,

00:46:36.462 --> 00:46:37.932
it was fancy, I treated myself to

00:46:37.932 --> 00:46:39.950
dinner at Nobu last night.

00:46:39.950 --> 00:46:41.377
And I was at the bar and

00:46:41.377 --> 00:46:43.200
I looked down at the Menu and

00:46:43.200 --> 00:46:46.020
I saw cocktails that look good.

00:46:46.020 --> 00:46:48.995
And then I saw the nonalcoholic

00:46:48.995 --> 00:46:50.259
user thing.

00:46:50.259 --> 00:46:51.793
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; It's $15 and

00:46:51.793 --> 00:46:52.778
I had three, and

00:46:52.778 --> 00:46:54.257
then I had a friend text,

00:46:54.257 --> 00:46:55.949
he's like you're in town,

00:46:55.949 --> 00:46:57.230
do you want to meet up?

00:46:57.230 --> 00:46:58.254
And I was like, I so

00:46:58.254 --> 00:46:59.470
badly want to meet up and

00:46:59.470 --> 00:47:00.110
I was like,

00:47:00.110 --> 00:47:01.582
I can't slept four hours,

00:47:01.582 --> 00:47:02.606
big day tomorrow,

00:47:02.606 --> 00:47:03.630
then I went home and

00:47:03.630 --> 00:47:05.231
I took the right medication,

00:47:05.231 --> 00:47:06.703
my doctor and I had a plan and

00:47:06.703 --> 00:47:08.650
I had an eight hour night.

00:47:08.650 --> 00:47:10.296
And I sent it to my family and

00:47:10.296 --> 00:47:11.734
my mom was like, good.

00:47:11.734 --> 00:47:13.682
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; So it's humbling to

00:47:13.682 --> 00:47:16.031
be in this kind of a dynamic, but

00:47:16.031 --> 00:47:18.381
I know that transparency with my

00:47:18.381 --> 00:47:20.817
loved ones is a critical part of my

00:47:20.817 --> 00:47:23.253
health and therefore theirs, so

00:47:23.253 --> 00:47:25.689
it's a pretty olympic regimen at

00:47:25.689 --> 00:47:27.460
this point.

00:47:27.460 --> 00:47:28.566
&gt;&gt; Well, it's great to hear that

00:47:28.566 --> 00:47:29.204
you have a process

00:47:29.204 --> 00:47:30.550
that helps you today.

00:47:30.550 --> 00:47:31.793
And whether you have bipolar

00:47:31.793 --> 00:47:32.569
disorder or not,

00:47:32.569 --> 00:47:34.560
mental hygiene is so important.

00:47:34.560 --> 00:47:35.912
And I know mental health is

00:47:35.912 --> 00:47:36.773
the focus of your

00:47:36.773 --> 00:47:38.430
newest venture pumpkin pie.

00:47:38.430 --> 00:47:39.142
So tell us a little bit

00:47:39.142 --> 00:47:40.260
more about that.

00:47:40.260 --> 00:47:42.558
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so pumpkin pie started as

00:47:42.558 --> 00:47:44.516
an idea to find a husband for

00:47:44.516 --> 00:47:47.411
my mother-in-law, who's amazing,

00:47:47.411 --> 00:47:49.090
she's amazing.

00:47:49.090 --> 00:47:51.120
And she's, how do I put this?

00:47:51.120 --> 00:47:52.802
She's very talkative, and

00:47:52.802 --> 00:47:55.790
boundaries are not her strong suit.

00:47:55.790 --> 00:47:58.432
So she's got a lot to offer and

00:47:58.432 --> 00:47:59.920
she's lonely.

00:47:59.920 --> 00:48:00.767
I think she'd be okay with

00:48:00.767 --> 00:48:01.310
me saying that.

00:48:01.310 --> 00:48:03.069
So, we started the company has

00:48:03.069 --> 00:48:04.895
an idea of how do we build a dating

00:48:04.895 --> 00:48:06.672
app for senior citizens

00:48:06.672 --> 00:48:08.688
I had been a founding investor and

00:48:08.688 --> 00:48:10.272
board member at Hinge, so

00:48:10.272 --> 00:48:12.000
I got to see the social mobile

00:48:12.000 --> 00:48:13.371
dating wars play out.

00:48:13.371 --> 00:48:15.443
And I remember thinking as I spent

00:48:15.443 --> 00:48:17.145
time entertaining the idea

00:48:17.145 --> 00:48:18.921
of helping my grandmother,

00:48:18.921 --> 00:48:20.401
I should just go with her

00:48:20.401 --> 00:48:21.668
on the dating apps.

00:48:21.668 --> 00:48:23.702
So I like took over her match

00:48:23.702 --> 00:48:24.501
account.

00:48:24.501 --> 00:48:25.468
Have you ever dated as

00:48:25.468 --> 00:48:26.714
a 75-year-old woman?

00:48:26.714 --> 00:48:30.008
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; The guys aren't

00:48:30.008 --> 00:48:31.545
the best at writing the profiles.

00:48:31.545 --> 00:48:35.283
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; And yet there's so

00:48:35.283 --> 00:48:37.192
much knowable at that stage

00:48:37.192 --> 00:48:38.941
about someone's life.

00:48:38.941 --> 00:48:40.668
All right, when you're 25, so

00:48:40.668 --> 00:48:42.037
much of your life stories in

00:48:42.037 --> 00:48:42.762
front of you.

00:48:42.762 --> 00:48:44.504
When you're 75, there's so

00:48:44.504 --> 00:48:45.407
many choices and

00:48:45.407 --> 00:48:46.962
experiences you've made.

00:48:46.962 --> 00:48:47.466
Have you spent

00:48:47.466 --> 00:48:48.726
your professional time whether you

00:48:48.726 --> 00:48:49.442
got married or not?

00:48:49.442 --> 00:48:50.945
Did you have kids?

00:48:50.945 --> 00:48:52.551
It's just amazing amount of newable

00:48:52.551 --> 00:48:54.002
information and it's not really

00:48:54.002 --> 00:48:55.359
there other than the profile.

00:48:55.359 --> 00:48:57.451
So we developed this.

00:48:57.451 --> 00:48:59.616
It's a game where you reveal your

00:48:59.616 --> 00:49:01.164
personality by opining

00:49:01.164 --> 00:49:02.720
on polarizing things.

00:49:02.720 --> 00:49:04.393
And then we hired a computer

00:49:04.393 --> 00:49:06.286
scientist from this school and

00:49:06.286 --> 00:49:07.741
I recruited a GSB friend

00:49:07.741 --> 00:49:09.141
to be my co-founder.

00:49:09.141 --> 00:49:11.329
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; Some lessons diehard.

00:49:11.329 --> 00:49:13.928
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; He's the founder and

00:49:13.928 --> 00:49:16.036
CEO of chess.com as his day job, so

00:49:16.036 --> 00:49:17.865
he's had an entertaining last

00:49:17.865 --> 00:49:19.003
couple of months.

00:49:19.003 --> 00:49:20.876
And we built the algorithm in

00:49:20.876 --> 00:49:21.734
the app, and

00:49:21.734 --> 00:49:23.764
it had zero predictive ability

00:49:23.764 --> 00:49:26.037
around romantic relationships.

00:49:26.037 --> 00:49:27.224
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; But

00:49:27.224 --> 00:49:28.592
the sample group we set

00:49:28.592 --> 00:49:30.537
it out to include people who were

00:49:30.537 --> 00:49:32.709
platonic friends with each other.

00:49:32.709 --> 00:49:34.459
And it was highly predictive of

00:49:34.459 --> 00:49:35.811
platonic friendship.

00:49:35.811 --> 00:49:37.340
And so that's when we said

00:49:37.340 --> 00:49:39.356
maybe we could build Tinder but for

00:49:39.356 --> 00:49:40.547
platonic friends.

00:49:40.547 --> 00:49:42.317
And the problem we've discovered

00:49:42.317 --> 00:49:42.988
with that and

00:49:42.988 --> 00:49:44.757
the reason it hasn't been built is

00:49:44.757 --> 00:49:46.161
that the two ingredients in

00:49:46.161 --> 00:49:47.871
a platonic friendship are mutual

00:49:47.871 --> 00:49:49.520
vulnerability in continuous,

00:49:49.520 --> 00:49:51.490
ongoing, unplanned interactions.

00:49:51.490 --> 00:49:52.272
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:49:52.272 --> 00:49:54.143
&gt;&gt; And so how do you plan off of

00:49:54.143 --> 00:49:56.698
an app an unplanned interaction and

00:49:56.698 --> 00:49:58.658
that's the conundrum that

00:49:58.658 --> 00:50:00.805
we're trying to transcend.

00:50:00.805 --> 00:50:02.596
&gt;&gt; We will stay tuned.

00:50:02.596 --> 00:50:04.357
Andy, I have one final question for

00:50:04.357 --> 00:50:05.238
you before we have

00:50:05.238 --> 00:50:06.540
a quick lightning round.

00:50:06.540 --> 00:50:07.556
This is a question that

00:50:07.556 --> 00:50:08.839
we're asking all of you from

00:50:08.839 --> 00:50:10.126
the top speakers this year.

00:50:10.126 --> 00:50:10.721
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:50:10.721 --> 00:50:12.386
&gt;&gt; Principled leadership is

00:50:12.386 --> 00:50:13.825
paramount at the GSB.

00:50:13.825 --> 00:50:15.573
What do you view as your role and

00:50:15.573 --> 00:50:17.592
leading with responsibility to not

00:50:17.592 --> 00:50:19.274
just your organization, but

00:50:19.274 --> 00:50:20.629
also society at large?

00:50:20.629 --> 00:50:23.261
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I love Ken Chenault,

00:50:23.261 --> 00:50:26.097
who ran American Express, one of

00:50:26.097 --> 00:50:29.137
the great black CEOs and pioneers,

00:50:29.137 --> 00:50:32.088
and he ran MX through many things.

00:50:32.088 --> 00:50:33.637
He was phenomenal CEO and

00:50:33.637 --> 00:50:35.046
also through 911.

00:50:35.046 --> 00:50:36.600
And coming out of 911,

00:50:36.600 --> 00:50:38.580
where some 50 American Express

00:50:38.580 --> 00:50:39.930
employees perished.

00:50:39.930 --> 00:50:41.715
He came out of that at some point

00:50:41.715 --> 00:50:43.500
and said that the job of a leader

00:50:43.500 --> 00:50:44.537
is just twofold.

00:50:44.537 --> 00:50:47.534
It's two bullets, two words each

00:50:47.534 --> 00:50:50.330
create hope, define reality.

00:50:50.330 --> 00:50:51.534
And I love that.

00:50:51.534 --> 00:50:53.839
And I think creating hope and

00:50:53.839 --> 00:50:56.146
defining reality are almost

00:50:56.146 --> 00:50:58.739
impossible to do at the same time

00:50:58.739 --> 00:51:01.731
because reality is scary and bleak.

00:51:01.731 --> 00:51:05.359
And then hope is something that we

00:51:05.359 --> 00:51:09.118
need to magnetize people to follow

00:51:09.118 --> 00:51:09.645
us.

00:51:09.645 --> 00:51:12.726
And we need to lead them somewhere

00:51:12.726 --> 00:51:13.720
winsome.

00:51:13.720 --> 00:51:14.357
And so

00:51:14.357 --> 00:51:17.927
I look at where we are socially and

00:51:17.927 --> 00:51:20.869
hope is hard at the moment.

00:51:20.869 --> 00:51:22.519
Hope is hard from a climate

00:51:22.519 --> 00:51:24.469
standpoint, hope is hard from

00:51:24.469 --> 00:51:26.494
a justice standpoint, whether

00:51:26.494 --> 00:51:29.044
racial gender, sexuality otherwise,

00:51:29.044 --> 00:51:30.844
hope is hard from a political

00:51:30.844 --> 00:51:32.656
divisiveness standpoint.

00:51:32.656 --> 00:51:35.518
There's a lot to not be hopeful

00:51:35.518 --> 00:51:36.292
about.

00:51:36.292 --> 00:51:37.024
And yet

00:51:37.024 --> 00:51:40.074
your job is to be hopeful and to

00:51:40.074 --> 00:51:43.734
convince other people to be hopeful

00:51:43.734 --> 00:51:47.273
to the people who will be following

00:51:47.273 --> 00:51:50.214
you in whatever you do next.

00:51:50.214 --> 00:51:51.943
And then your job is also

00:51:51.943 --> 00:51:54.363
to not let those people down by not

00:51:54.363 --> 00:51:56.957
being realistic about what you're

00:51:56.957 --> 00:51:58.004
trying to do.

00:51:58.004 --> 00:52:00.682
And doing those things together

00:52:00.682 --> 00:52:03.260
it takes a rare human that can do

00:52:03.260 --> 00:52:05.838
both of those things and many of

00:52:05.838 --> 00:52:08.725
those rare humans are right here.

00:52:08.725 --> 00:52:10.284
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, well, thank you.

00:52:10.284 --> 00:52:12.211
That's a great note to end on.

00:52:12.211 --> 00:52:13.508
We'll do a quick lightning round

00:52:13.508 --> 00:52:14.811
now just in the interest of time.

00:52:14.811 --> 00:52:16.353
This is a view from the top

00:52:16.353 --> 00:52:17.168
tradition.

00:52:17.168 --> 00:52:18.218
So Andy, I'll ask you four

00:52:18.218 --> 00:52:19.054
questions and- &gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:52:19.054 --> 00:52:20.038
&gt;&gt; Please say whatever comes

00:52:20.038 --> 00:52:20.623
to mind first.

00:52:20.623 --> 00:52:21.276
&gt;&gt; Okay, I'm ready.

00:52:21.276 --> 00:52:21.904
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:52:21.904 --> 00:52:22.722
[INAUDIBLE].

00:52:22.722 --> 00:52:23.960
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah.

00:52:23.960 --> 00:52:25.796
So the first one is best piece of

00:52:25.796 --> 00:52:27.215
advice ever received.

00:52:27.215 --> 00:52:28.728
&gt;&gt; You catch more flies with honey.

00:52:28.728 --> 00:52:30.505
&gt;&gt; Nice [LAUGH].

00:52:30.505 --> 00:52:32.067
Next time the Cubs will win

00:52:32.067 --> 00:52:33.204
the World Series.

00:52:33.204 --> 00:52:34.765
&gt;&gt; 2034.

00:52:34.765 --> 00:52:35.679
&gt;&gt; Wow, okay?

00:52:35.679 --> 00:52:40.102
[SOUND] Most memorable GSB class.

00:52:40.102 --> 00:52:41.274
&gt;&gt; Interpersonal dynamics.

00:52:41.274 --> 00:52:43.091
&gt;&gt; Yeah, fan favorite [SOUND].

00:52:43.091 --> 00:52:46.231
Favorite bonobo is pants print.

00:52:46.231 --> 00:52:47.059
&gt;&gt; The Pink Panthers,

00:52:47.059 --> 00:52:48.623
before the trademark, shut us down,

00:52:48.623 --> 00:52:51.961
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; Wish we had a model

00:52:51.961 --> 00:52:53.373
wearing that today.

00:52:53.373 --> 00:52:54.941
Andy, you didn't give your talk

00:52:54.941 --> 00:52:56.453
here on campus when you're a GSB

00:52:56.453 --> 00:52:58.077
student, but thank you so much for

00:52:58.077 --> 00:52:58.805
coming back and

00:52:58.805 --> 00:53:00.102
sharing your story today.

00:53:00.102 --> 00:53:01.256
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thank you.

00:53:01.256 --> 00:53:07.731
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:53:07.731 --> 00:53:08.547
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much, Andy.

00:53:08.547 --> 00:53:09.094
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:53:09.094 --> 00:53:09.929
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much.

00:53:09.929 --> 00:53:11.457
